k this had been factored in  in recent days we'd seen the police  getting more aggressive and using more  violent tactics towards women after a  period  where as you might remember there had  been slightly  lighter touch towards female protesters  we'd seen  protests where women would stand in  front of men who are protesting or  journalists who are said to be arrested  and  preventing their arrest by standing  between them and the police that is now  definitely over  but interestingly enough those those  arrests came right at the beginning and  after that  after about five minutes of shock and  confusion people  the women out demonstrating did manage  to get regroup and  spent the next two hours protesting  walking through most of the city centre  getting their message and getting a  whole lot of support from people on the  streets clapping them  beeping their horns i think the example  of maria kalesnikova who was abducted  here in minsk last week and who  successfully resisted the attempt by  authorities to throw her out of her own  country by ripping her passport jumping  out of a car window that has definitely  galvanized many here in minsk but nick  where does where do things go from here  i mean lukashenko is still refusing  to meet with the opposition how does the  opposition intend to proceed  that's uh the million-dollar question i  think there's no clear answer to that  this is  unknown territory for a country which  basically for quarter century had no  politics  in which one man called all the big  shots i think this is a kind of cultural  protest now where protests spread beyond  the city streets beyond the sunday  demonstrations i was  in a residential district of minsk just  yesterday evening  in a yard building residential building  where there's been a mural  of some djs who basically came up with  the soundtrack to these protests  that mural has been painted over time  and time again by the authorities here  and time and time again the people  living there  have replaced the mural so that now the  police is there 24 hours a day  essentially guarding a wall to prevent  people from replacing a mural  and the the people who live in that yard  are out talking to the police meeting  every evening drinking tea  there was even an impromptu concert so  there's a level to which the more the  government clamps down the more they're  actually forcing people  closer to one another bringing them  closer and actually encouraging to come  out to protest just out of  out of a spirit of resistance to these  attempts to really clamp down on their  everyday life and their surroundings  well more protests are planned for today  so did yesterday's violence then not  intimidate people are we expecting  a large turnout today  well definitely that arrest of marijuana  her disappearance rather last week is  definitely  uh something that has angered and  shocked many who thought they'd seen  everything here so these streets where  i'm saying now look set to be  a host to tens if not hundreds of  thousands of people later today  um we've seen further warnings from the  government basically trying to  make people think again about coming out  reminding people that any  uh participation in this kind of  demonstration is illegal and will have  consequences for them  so far we've seen those threats aren't  working all right dw's mcconnelly  reporting for us from minsk thank you
 

